WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
Web has imaged a giant planet 7.6 times Jupiter's mass, just 12 light years away. This is fascinating Frontiers, episode 234. Station crews finish the last solar array upgrades. Autom foliage is mapped from orbit and cosmic spotlight. Spectral data from the same capture indicate water ice as a cloud constituent in that atmosphere. Futura reports the observations suggest possible water ice clouds. The planet lies close enough for detailed atmospheric study with current

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.914
instruments. Crew members will conduct space walks on October 29th and November 5th on

00:00:35.914 --> 00:00:41.134
the International Space Station, or ISS. A report on Reddit says the outings will complete

00:00:41.134 --> 00:00:46.414
installation of the station's last two new solar arrays. These final arrays bring the total

00:00:46.414 --> 00:00:51.954
upgraded power capacity to its planned level. The schedule aligns with current station operations

00:00:51.954 --> 00:00:58.067
and crew availability. The ocean color instrument on the PACE satellite tracks vegetation redness

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:03.687
using 286 spectral bands. According to the National Aeronautics and Space Administration,

00:01:04.107 --> 00:01:10.567
or NASA, the instrument distinguishes anthocyanins in red leaves from carotenoids and chlorophylls.

00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:15.907
Daily global coverage lets the timing of color chain to be compared across entire continents.

00:01:16.627 --> 00:01:20.907
Agricultural fields appear in the maps when harvest alters pigment ratios differently

00:01:20.907 --> 00:01:26.447
from natural autumn foliage. The satellite observes both oceans and land vegetation at these

00:01:26.447 --> 00:01:32.154
wavelengths each day. The Milky Way disc shows younger stars concentrated in ever thinner

00:01:32.154 --> 00:01:37.854
layers above and below the midplane. Fizz.org reports this pattern indicates the disc grew

00:01:37.854 --> 00:01:43.094
upside down over time. Older stellar populations occupy thicker vertical distributions,

00:01:43.614 --> 00:01:48.814
while newer stars settle into narrower bands. The observation comes from detailed mapping of

00:01:48.814 --> 00:01:54.671
stellar ages and positions within the galactic disc. Astronomy picture of the day features a deep

00:01:54.671 --> 00:01:59.651
view of the Orion Nebula, revealing glowing gas and dust sheets around the trapezium

00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:00.331
star cluster.

00:02:00.951 --> 00:02:06.731
The Nebula lies 1,500 light-years away and spans roughly 40 light-years according to NASA.

00:02:07.391 --> 00:02:11.391
It contains proplids and stellar jets within an active star-forming region.

00:02:11.991 --> 00:02:16.531
The great Nebula in Orion sits near the easily visible belt stars in the constellation.

00:02:17.271 --> 00:02:21.971
Emission in hydrogen highlights the wisps of dust and gas, and the featured deep image

00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:24.391
assigns colors to highlight that hydrogen emission.

00:02:24.791 --> 00:02:27.931
The region forms part of the same spiral arm as the sun.

00:02:28.571 --> 00:02:32.091
Many stellar nurseries inside the nebula continue to produce new stars.

00:02:33.185 --> 00:02:38.265
The full moon of October 26th carries the name Hunter's Moon, tied to historical hunting

00:02:38.265 --> 00:02:40.225
practices that needed bright moonlight.

00:02:40.845 --> 00:02:46.085
Space.com reports the moon appears full on the night of the 26th across most longitudes.

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:49.105
Observers can watch it rise in the evening sky.

00:02:49.105 --> 00:02:54.585
The name traces back to seasonal activities when extra nighttime illumination aided hunters.

00:02:55.572 --> 00:03:01.192
Giovanni Cassini used a 34-foot refractor at the Paris Observatory to find Saturn's moon,

00:03:01.712 --> 00:03:09.272
Yapetus on October 25, 1671. The find, reported by astronomy, made Yapetus the second Saturnian

00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:15.712
moon identified after Titan. Cassini had arrived as Observatory Director two years earlier. The

00:03:15.712 --> 00:03:18.832
The discovery followed acquisition of the large campaigning instrument.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.732
The moon now marks 355 years since that detection.

00:03:23.715 --> 00:03:28.395
The European Space Agency, or ESA, has set a press conference at its headquarters in

00:03:28.395 --> 00:03:32.055
Paris on November 2nd at 1130 Central European time.

00:03:32.935 --> 00:03:35.335
Journalists can meet astronaut Sophie Adinot there.

00:03:35.975 --> 00:03:39.675
This marks her first in-person press conference since returning from the ISS.

00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:43.495
Accreditation remains open until October 29th.

00:03:43.495 --> 00:03:48.195
The event offers direct access to Adinot following her recent ISS mission.

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:52.155
The briefing is scheduled specifically for media accreditation holders.

00:03:53.131 --> 00:03:58.631
In brief, a Reddit user posted a 75 shot composite of last month's full moon, taken

00:03:58.631 --> 00:04:05.511
September 28th with an Nikon D5300 and a 300-millimeter lens, then processed through

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:10.591
auto-stackered and GIMP after best frame selection retained 72% of the exposures.

00:04:10.591 --> 00:04:16.231
A Toronto observer collected 2.5 hours of data on the Rosette Nebula under Bordel-8

00:04:16.231 --> 00:04:24.971
skies, with an SV-Bony 122-millimeter refractor and a ZW-002-600MC Pro camera, stacking

00:04:24.971 --> 00:04:30.651
an astropixel processor and continuing in-pixen sight for background extraction, noise reduction

00:04:30.651 --> 00:04:32.371
and narrow-band normalization.

00:04:32.891 --> 00:04:37.331
A beginner posted a first smartphone moon photo taken through a small telescope with

00:04:37.331 --> 00:04:42.291
smartphone adapter, adjusting brightness to manage the moon's intensity during the exposure.

00:04:43.051 --> 00:04:46.711
Autumn color on those pace maps migrates equatorward week by week.

00:04:47.431 --> 00:04:52.451
Maps covering September 29th through October 4th put the peak redness over northern Canada,

00:04:52.931 --> 00:04:59.211
Alaska, Mongolia, and Russia. A week later those reddish zones had moved south into the mid-Atlantic,

00:04:59.811 --> 00:05:05.211
southern Russia, and northern China. Northern Tundra regions contribute intense reds from blueberries,

00:05:05.211 --> 00:05:11.052
trees, sages, and mosses. Many of the reddest forest patches feature sugar maples and red

00:05:11.052 --> 00:05:16.392
maples in the Adirondacks, the Green Mountains, and the White Mountains. Future seasons will

00:05:16.392 --> 00:05:20.612
show whether the observed shifts repeat or vary with temperature and drought patterns.

00:05:21.332 --> 00:05:26.812
Color on this planet moves across distances that dwarf any one forest. The same daily stream

00:05:26.812 --> 00:05:31.672
traces a seasonal wave across thousands of kilometers. No ground team could sample

00:05:31.672 --> 00:05:33.232
enough sites to build that picture.

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:38.752
During the week of October 5th to October 11th, redness began to fade in New England and

00:05:38.752 --> 00:05:41.832
upstate New York, even as it spread across much of the Midwest.

00:05:42.472 --> 00:05:45.792
The second map also shows more color in the US southeast.

00:05:46.452 --> 00:05:50.972
Smaller patches of red are visible in Australia in that same early October window.

00:05:51.592 --> 00:05:56.692
Particularly high redness showed up in parts of Northern and Eastern Russia, Northern Alaska

00:05:56.692 --> 00:06:00.192
and Northern Canada from September 29th to October 4th.

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:05.452
Clouds produce what appear to be gaps in the vegetation, so the global views need careful

00:06:05.452 --> 00:06:05.832
reading.

00:06:06.372 --> 00:06:09.092
Not all of the yellows and reds on the maps are fall leaves.

00:06:09.872 --> 00:06:15.232
Crops in some agricultural areas show high anthocyanin levels as they age and after they are harvested.

00:06:15.912 --> 00:06:20.452
Fall color is tracked by measuring how much red, orange, and yellow forests show compared

00:06:20.452 --> 00:06:20.972
with green.

00:06:21.692 --> 00:06:25.912
Tracking that ratio over time reveals where leaves are changing and when color peaks.

00:06:25.912 --> 00:06:32.072
areas shown in green have values near one, meaning the satellite detects about as much red as green,

00:06:32.572 --> 00:06:37.832
and most leaves have not yet turned. Areas shown in light yellow and red have higher values,

00:06:38.312 --> 00:06:43.732
indicating progressively more red pigment relative to green. A hyperspectral look captures subtle

00:06:43.732 --> 00:06:49.320
pigment shifts that single band or three band cameras would miss. The Japanese tradition of

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:55.120
Momi Jigari stretches back more than a millennium. American mentions of foliage trips began appearing in

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:00.800
the 19th century. Blueberries are not the whole northern story. Billberries, grasses, and

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.280
mosses in marshy tundra add to those early red peaks. The maps still leave open why certain

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:11.140
tundra regions read and earlier than expected. They also leave open whether the timing will

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:16.820
drift as average temperatures rise. We can watch the planet repaint itself from orbit, but the

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:22.380
precise triggers that set one forest ahead of another remain unresolved. October 29th brings

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.880
the first of the two station spacewalks that finished the Array work.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.220
After November 2nd in Paris, Adonaut's first in-person account since landing will be on

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:31.640
the record.

